; test/bl/specter_compat_test.bl — the Specter compatibility measurement,
; ported from test/beam_lisp/specter_compat_test.exs.
;
; The thesis (unchanged from the original): Specter is beam-lisp's north star
; for optics. beam-lisp ships its own optics in priv/std/optics.bl, but the
; honest question is how far beam-lisp is from running Clojure's Specter
; as-is. Every fixture under test/fixtures/specter/ is a verbatim block of the
; real Specter, copied with its sha256 in a header; the harness wraps each in a
; throwaway `(ns …)` and evaluates it.
;
; ONLY slices that load AND behave correctly are exercised. A slice that needs
; a local edit is a FAIL, recorded in docs/specter-compat.md — never patched
; into passing. The measurement (wave 28) is 25 of 31 load, and 9 of 31 load
; AND behave (slices 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 12, 16, 17, 31); the nine that behave
; are exercised, plus the three engine slices (06, 07, 14) that run against
; beam-lisp's ported engine. The remaining load failures (11, 15, 25, 26, 27,
; 29) are Specter-internal (i/direct-nav-obj, n/PosNavigator, eachnav,
; srange-transform*) or a correct reader-conditional miss (29). The gap between
; loading and behaving is the point: what remains is Specter's impl machinery
; (i/NONE, the compiled path cache, the exec interop, .select* host dispatch)
; rather than beam-lisp core forms. docs/specter-compat.md holds the full
; per-slice table and the honest re-ranking.
;
; ── coverage map (ExUnit test → BL deftest), all 13 originals, none dropped ──
;   vendored-slices-are-byte-for-byte-upstream (checksum, no local edits)
;     → vendored-slices-are-byte-for-byte-upstream-checksum-no-local-edits (31)
;   verbatim specter slices:
;     extract-basic-filter-fn normalizes a filter path into a predicate
;       → extract-basic-filter-fn-normalizes-a-filter-path-into-a-predicate  (4)
;     rich-navigator protocol defines and dispatches a working navigator
;       → rich-navigator-protocol-defines-and-dispatches-a-working-navigator (1)
;     collector and implicit-nav protocols define and dispatch
;       → collector-and-implicit-nav-protocols-define-and-dispatch           (1)
;     insert-before-index-list inserts a value at an index into a seq
;       → insert-before-index-list-inserts-a-value-at-an-index-into-a-seq    (3)
;     determine-params-impls groups method impls into a method-name keyed map
;       → determine-params-impls-groups-method-impls-into-a-method-name-keyed-map (1)
;     nav/richnav macros build a working navigator (empty params -> reify path)
;       → nav-richnav-macros-build-a-working-navigator-empty-params-reify-path (1)
;     defnav/defrichnav macros build a navigator (empty params, for+vary-meta+declare)
;       → defnav-defrichnav-macros-build-a-navigator-empty-params (1 + nav metadata)
;     dynamic-param? classifies the path-analyzer records via type
;       → dynamic-param-classifies-the-path-analyzer-records-via-type        (1)
;     static-path? distinguishes static paths from dynamic-path records
;       → static-path-distinguishes-static-paths-from-dynamic-path-records   (1)
;   verbatim slices running on beam-lisp's ported engine:
;     slice 07: all-select folds with doseqres, honouring NONE and reduced
;       → slice-07-all-select-folds-with-doseqres-honouring-none-and-reduced (5)
;     slice 06: selected?* answers through compiled-select-any*
;       → slice-06-selected-answers-through-compiled-select-any             (4)
;     slice 14: do-keypath-transform rebuilds the map around the new value
;       → slice-14-do-keypath-transform-rebuilds-the-map-around-the-new-value (2)
;
; Adaptations, and why:
;   * `BeamLisp.Vector.new([…])` disappears: on the BL side a vector literal IS
;     the vector, so `BeamLisp.Vector.new([42, :ok])` is written `[42 :ok]`.
;   * `%{a: 2, b: 9}` (an Elixir map literal) is the BL map literal
;     `{:a 2 :b 9}` — the same map on both sides of `=`.
;   * `BeamLisp.Test.realize/1` has no BL twin; the private list helper's lazy
;     result is forced with `vec`, which realizes it to the same vector.
;   * `setup_all` (init) has no ward equivalent, so `(BeamLisp/init)` runs at
;     file top level, exactly as the wave-25/28 ports do.
;   * `async: false` has no BL equivalent: `bl test` isolates every FILE in its
;     own fork by default, which is strictly stronger than an `async: false`
;     ExUnit case.
;   * The nav metadata `defnav`/`defrichnav` attach (`:arglists`, via
;     `vary-meta`) is asserted where the original only documented it, so the
;     macro's second output — the navigator's var metadata — is covered too.
;
; Run: bin/bl test test/bl/specter_compat_test.bl

(ns specter.compat.test)

(BeamLisp/init)

; Provenance of the vendored source — src/clj/com/rpl/specter/* in
; https://github.com/redplanetlabs/specter. License Apache-2.0.
(def commit "6119462a4d959834f2d78a6183d74608bf08ab52")

; All vendored fixtures: [fixture upstream-file start-line end-line
; sha256-of-code-portion]. The sha256 is of the fixture with its `;` header
; comments removed — i.e. exactly the upstream text (the `(ns …)` header line
; is excluded because the harness provides its own). If any fixture drifts from
; upstream, the checksum test below fails and the fidelity claim is void. Every
; fixture is vendored and checksummed; only the ones that genuinely load AND
; behave are additionally exercised as tests.
(def fixtures
  [["01_rich_navigator_protocol" "protocols.cljc" 3 18
    "e4c40a7ba3c73bbd07e9a636b8bc95be1a70c29cbddc9bd2eba936136b12e243"]
   ["02_collector_implicitnav" "protocols.cljc" 21 27
    "7ff3b4873d36a0e8ccf6b91006653b4e611f0869575ce6abf9c5332ee72b238c"]
   ["03_determine_params_impls" "macros.clj" 6 11
    "66080e990590d5bf800d3e7105e8611cb135d472b2363517f0d165bf329ca9f0"]
   ["04_richnav_nav" "macros.clj" 14 32
    "8ee194989f4b2ecd349b4325d5e1d8f120480282da48aa582ded61887845d2cf"]
